\f1\fs22 Agent Ajent Jones trusted Gerry Hardman\'97Commissioner of Police, acting Director of Freedom, acting Commissioner of Elvi Rights\'97not one iota, not one jot from one of his many, mostly snapped, HB pencils.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 He was convinced Gerry was Jihad Man, and Jihad Man lived in his imagination as everything loathable about the things he loathed most: political climbers, terrorists and people who liked cats. He was sure Gerry would be the kind of person who would kick a dog, stroke a cat, and threaten the security of the Great Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia. And not by honourable means, of course, oh no! Not by good, honest, direct maiming and killing, but by means of subterfuge, by the use of pretty words and poisons, under the cloak of shadows. Just take the digging operation he had overseen, under the guise of building and construction, conducted mostly at night: it had recently been suspended with not a foundation poured, a brick laid, nor a steel girder set reaching for the sky. There were rumours Jihad Man was breaking into ancient labs looking for bio-weapons long since decreed to be too dangerous, even in the most trustworthy hands. Yet Jihad Man\'92s hands were probably already deploying those insidious, invisible bio-weapons in ways a good, honest, PAPER gunship could not be deployed. They would kill people ingloriously, not by such plain, honest ordnance like sharp blades or particle weapons.\
Yet there Agent Jones was, doing some kind of underhanded sabotage, designed to strike at the Elvi, to incite conflict among them, or conflict between the Elvi and decent society, a society of order where his kind of order-restoring skills were highly valued. If such was the case, then he was all for it.\
At least for now.\
And so Agent Jones quickly and efficiently executed his mission until only one enemy target remained: a place called the Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill. In the early hours of the morning between closing time and dawn, Agent Jones snarled as he ordered his team in.\
They were quiet; they were professional. They carried crates of assets they were to deploy, and this time Agent Jones decided he would deploy them himself. That\'92s how Agent Jones came to be standing before a \'93Bubbler\'94\'97a faithful reproduction of a 1946 Wurlitzer 1015 jukebox\'97with two crates containing a few dozen 45-rpm gospel records. He sneered at it as it bathed him in garish neon light. He and his team had practised swapping records on this exact model, as they had practised on many other models popular in the Ghetto\'97the Rock-Ola Model 1438, the Seeburg LPC-1 Select-O-Matic, and the AMI Continental 2 among them. Agent Jones was nothing if not thorough.\
He worked quickly. Without taking the precaution of switching the power off, he opened the machine\'92s front door, released the carousel lock, and plucked records at random, sliding replacements from his crates into place, checking they aligned in their slots and fitted securely. Even in the dim light, he recognised some of the song titles from his practice runs. He sneered at most of them\'97
\f2\i How Great Thou Art
\f1\i0 ; 
\f2\i Amazing Grace
\f1\i0 ; 
\f2\i In the Garden
\f1\i0 ; 
\f2\i Glory, Glory to South Sydney
\f1\i0 ; 
\f2\i Precious Lord, Take My Hand
\f1\i0 .\
One title struck him as different from the others: 
\f2\i You\'92ll Never Walk Alone
\f1\i0 . He\'92d never heard any of them but, just from the title, that one seemed different. Agent Jones consulted his list of slot numbers, and pressed buttons on the jukebox\'92s console. He watched through the glass as a gripper arm clutched a record, and rotated it for placement on the turntable. The tonearm swung out and lowered its stylus onto the record with a gentle thud and a slightly louder crackle.\
Music always startled Agent Jones, and the jukebox was loud. Panicked, he searched for a 
\fs20 CANCEL
\fs22  button on the machine\'92s console. Finding none, he bashed the glass with his fist, cursed, and was about to wrench the power cord from the wall when he realised doing so while the jukebox was in motion might make a noise his training had taught him not to make. He couldn\'92t risk it. He could just leave, and let the record play out, but indecision gripped him. Agent Ajent Jones resolved to wait the record out, to make sure everything would be normal and quiet before calling the mission complete.\
He stood leaning on the machine, grinding his teeth against its cacophonous vibrations until, some three minutes later, he exhaled with relief as peaceful silence was restored.\
A curious thing happened. Something in Ajent\'92s heart longed for\'85 what was it? The silence sounded empty and, somehow, lonely. The corners of his mouth twitched as he decided to test another record. He consulted his list of slot numbers and punched another into the console. This time he was prepared for the volume. The song was gentler\'97softer strings, less strident vocals. The lyrics\'97cheesy, certainly\'97spoke of redemption, of hope and encouragement. When the song ended, it seemed only reasonable that Ajent test another. His bottom lip trembled. The lights of the jukebox shone upon him with an ethereal glow, with an eternal peace. Yes, it would be all right to test another record.\
The next song spoke of social justice and equality, and he hated it.\
Well, he couldn\'92t leave after that, could he? So he selected another record.\
\'93Sir?\'94 One of the team had crept up behind him. \'93Shouldn\'92t we be leaving?\'94\
Agent Jones regarded the junior agent with contempt. \'93I will test the machine, and you will return to your post,\'94 he said, his tone constructed of tempered steel.\
\'93Yes sir,\'94 the agent said, abashed, and slunk into the murky darkness of the room.\
Agent Jones rankled. He tightened his pink tie so tight, it nearly cut off the blood to his head. His mood ruined, he\'92d need another record to restore it.\
If he\'92d understood anything of music, Ajent might have known the next song was in 12/8 time and paced at a little over eighty beats per minute\'97perfectly designed to pull at your heart strings. \'93Don\'92t be afraid of the dark,\'94 Ajent sang atonally, mimicking what he\'92d heard a second before. His bulky, violent frame sagged as he put more of his weight on the jukebox with his arms. He needed the support. He couldn\'92t hold his head up. \'93Walk on,\'94 he sang, but he wouldn\'92t be walking anywhere. His knees shook, and it seemed the only thing to do would be turn and sit on the floor, to lean against the warming machine. \'93For my dreams\'85\'94 he sang, and realised he never dreamed of anything. He\'92d never hoped. His whole life was storms and strife.\
The song finished, but the echo reverberated in his undernourished soul. Ajent planted his elbows on his knees and held his head in his hands, and blubbered. He blew his nose on his pink tie, and cursed.\
\'93Why am I wearing a pink tie?\'94 he demanded of everyone not present. There were black ties and red ties and green ties and blue ties, he knew. \'93I should be wearing a black tie,\'94 he said to the same no ones not present as before. And then he cried free-flowing tears of loss and misery, for all the things denied him in life, for all the gaps and events he no longer recalled, the deprivation of decades, the cost of which he was only now counting.
